title: >-
  rain-corr-geva-bayes: Rainfall Correlation and Generalized
  Extreme Value Analysis using Bayesian Inference -
  Application to Grenada
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite it using the
  metadata from this file.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Aaron Jerome
    family-names: Rampersad
    email: aaron.rampersad@pg.canterbury.ac.nz
    affiliation: University of Canterbury
    orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0009-0009-6134-3828'
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: 10.5281/zenodo.20249250
repository-code: 'https://github.com/AaronJR474/rain-corr-geva-bayes'
abstract: >-
  This repository provides a reproducible workflow for
  analysing fragmented daily rainfall records in
  data-limited settings, with application to Grenada. It
  implements a site-specific correlation framework to
  diagnose the spatial structure and relative
  non-stationarity of daily rainfall, and uses Bayesian
  stationary and non-stationary spatial correlation models
  within ordinary kriging for rainfall imputation. For
  comparison, simpler deterministic imputation approaches
  are also evaluated, including nearest-neighbour distance
  (NND) and inverse-distance weighting (IDW), the latter
  implemented through a linear radial basis function. The
  imputed rainfall archive is then used within Bayesian
  generalized extreme value (GEV) and generalized Pareto
  (GPD) analyses to estimate rainfall extremes and develop
  multi-station intensity-duration-frequency (IDF)
  relationships.
keywords:
  - Bayesian inference
  - spatial correlation modelling
  - rainfall non-stationarity
  - data imputation
  - extreme rainfall
  - intensity-duration-frequency curves
  - small island developing states
  - Grenada
